We have developed a comprehensive method for determining stellar parameters, including surface gravity, effective temperature, and metallicity, using high-resolution ultraviolet spectra. This method is particularly effective for M-dwarf stars, which are challenging to study due to their faint UV emission. We applied this method to a sample of 25 M-dwarf field stars, analyzing their UV spectra and comparing our results to those obtained from other methods. Our analysis revealed that our UV-based method yields accurate surface gravity and metallicity values, with a median difference of 0.1 dex compared to isochrone-based values. However, we found that our UV-based method tends to overestimate the effective temperature by 100-200 K. We also found that our UV-based method is more sensitive to the presence of magnetic activity, which can lead to inaccurate results. We plan to apply this method to a larger sample of M-dwarf stars in the future. 

